rm a stop codon (TAA/TAG/TGA). If yes, tag_ok = False (contains stop codon at terminus). If no, tag_ok = True.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether to use the literal last three nucleotides or the in-frame last codon. Since both agree here, I'll go with a robust implementation. Let me use the literal last three nucleotides for the \"stop codon at the terminus\" check in tag_ok, because that's the most literal reading of \"at the terminus.\" But actually, for correctness with respect to the biology of C-terminal tags, an in-frame stop codon is what matters. But since they agree here, let me not overthink. Let me use: terminal stop codon check = (last three nucleotides form a stop codon). This is simple and literal.\n\nHmm, but actually, I realize there might be an issue. Let me reconsider c02's stop_ok and tag_ok more carefully, because c02 is the tricky one.\n\nc02: ATGGCCGACTACAAAGACGATGACGACAAGAA, length 32.\n- start_ok: starts with ATG → True.\n- stop_ok: ends with in-frame stop codon? Length 32, not divisible by 3 → False. (Also, last three = GAA, not a stop codon.)\n- frame_ok: length divisible by 3? No (32). → False.\n- tag_ok: C_terminal_FLAG. Does the insert end with a stop codon? Last three = GAA, not a stop codon → no stop codon at terminus → tag_ok = True.\n\nSo c02: start T, stop F, frame F, tag T. overall = fail. issues = STOP, FRAME.\n\nOK that's consistent with what I had. Good.\n\nNow let me reconsider: are there any constructs that pass?\n- c01: fails on TAG.\n- c02: fails on STOP and FRAME.\n- c03: fails on FRAME and TAG.\n\nNone pass. All fail. That's the result.\n\nWait, let me reconsider whether I'm being too strict. Let me reconsider c02's stop_ok. The rule says \"the insert ends in an in-frame stop codon (TAA, TAG, or TGA).\" c02 ends with \"...AAGAA\". The last three nucleotides are \"GAA\". Not a stop codon. And length is not divisible by 3. So stop_ok = False. Definitely.\n\nAnd frame_ok for c02: length 32 not divisible by 3 → False