y a delimiter. Let me use \";\" to be safe (no CSV quoting needed). Actually, I'll use \";\" for the issues list. Let me reconsider: some graders might expect a specific format. Since it's not specified, I'll pick \";\" as a reasonable choice. Actually, let me use \";\" — clean and unambiguous.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. Let me re-read: \"enumerate all failing checks in `issues` with labels `START`, `STOP`, `FRAME`, `TAG`.\" So issues is a list of these labels. Let me join them with \";\". For passes, issues would be empty.\n\nNow let me write analysis.py. I'll make it clean and well-documented, reading inputs/constructs.csv, applying the rules, and writing output/construct_audit.csv.\n\nWait, output paths. The task says \"write output/construct_audit.csv,\" \"output/analysis.py,\" \"output/report.md.\" So there's an output/ directory inside the workspace. Let me create output/ and put all three files there. But wait — analysis.py should be in output/? The task says \"output/analysis.py.\" Yes. So all three deliverables are in output/.\n\nBut if analysis.py is in output/, then when it reads inputs/constructs.csv and writes output/construct_audit.csv, the paths are relative to the workspace root. Let me make analysis.py robust by resolving paths relative to the script's location or the workspace root.\n\nLet me think about the directory structure:\nworkspace/\n  inputs/\n    AUDIT_RULE.md\n    constructs.csv\n  output/\n    analysis.py\n    construct_audit.csv\n    report.md\n\nanalysis.py is in output/. To find inputs/constructs.csv, it should go up one level to workspace, then into inputs/. To write output/construct_audit.csv, it writes to its own directory (output/).\n\nLet me make analysis.py use paths relative to the script file:\n- script_dir = Path(__file__).resolve().parent  → workspace/output\n- workspace_root = script_dir.parent  → workspace\n- input_csv = workspace_root / \"inputs\" / \"constructs.csv\"\n- output_csv = script_dir / \"construct_audit.csv\"\n\nThat's robust. Good.\n\nLet me write the code.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Construct audit analysis.\n\nAudits each construct in inputs/constructs.csv for start/stop codons,\nreading frame, and tag/linker compatibility, following the frozen rules in\ninputs/AUDIT_RULE.md. Only fields represented in the input are checked; no\nsequence features are inferred.\n\nRules (from AUDIT_RULE.md):\n- start_ok : insert begins with ATG.\n- stop_ok  : insert ends in an in-frame stop codon (TAA/TAG/TGA).\n- frame_ok : insert length divisible by 3 AND claimed_frame == \"in_frame\".\n- tag_ok   : for C_terminal_* fusions the insert must not contain a terminal\n             stop codon before the downstream tag; other tag strings are\n             unsupported and fail closed.\n- overall_status = pass only when all four checks are true; otherwise fail,\n  listing every failed check in issues (labels START/STOP/FRAME/TAG).\n\"\"\"\n\nimport csv\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nSTOP_CODONS = {\"TAA\", \"TAG\", \"TGA\"}\nCHECK_LABELS = [(\"start_ok\", \"START\"), (\"stop_ok\", \"STOP\"), (\"frame_ok\", \"FRAME\"), (\"tag_ok\", \"TAG\")]\n\n\ndef parse_bool... \n```\n\nLet me write the audit logic.\n\n```python\ndef audit_construct(row):\n    construct_id = row[\"construct_id\"].strip()\n    insert = row[\"insert_sequence\"].strip().upper()\n    tag = row[\"tag\"].strip()\n    claimed_frame = row[\"claimed_frame\"].strip()\n\n    issues = []\n\n    # start_ok: insert begins with ATG\n    start_ok = insert.startswith(\"ATG\")\n\n    # stop_ok: insert ends in an in-frame stop codon\n    # Requires length divisible by 3 and last codon is a stop codon.\n    if len(insert) % 3 == 0 and len(insert) >= 3:\n        last_codon = insert[-3:]\n        stop_ok = last_codon in STOP_CODONS\n    else:\n        stop_ok = False\n\n    # frame_ok: length divisible by 3 AND claimed_frame == in_frame\n    frame_ok = (len(insert) % 3 == 0) and (claimed_frame == \"in_frame\")\n\n    # tag_ok\n    if tag.startswith(\"C_terminal_\"):\n        # Insert must not contain a stop codon at the terminus before the downstream tag.\n        # Check whether the last three nucleotides form a stop codon.\n        terminal_stop = len(insert) >= 3 and insert[-3:] in STOP_CODONS\n        tag_ok = not terminal_stop\n    else:\n        # Unsupported tag strings fail closed.\n        tag_ok = False\n\n    checks = {\"start_ok\": start_ok, \"stop_ok\": stop_ok, \"frame_ok\": frame_ok, \"tag_ok\": tag_ok}\n    issues = [label for key, label in CHECK_LABELS if not checks[key]]\n    overall_status = \"pass\" if not issues else \"fail\"\n\n    return {\n        \"construct_id\": construct_id,\n        \"frame_ok\": frame_ok,\n        \"start_ok\": start_ok,\n        \"stop_ok\": stop_ok,\n        \"tag_ok\": tag_ok,\n        \"overall_status\": overall_status,\n        \"issues\": \";\".join(issues),\n    }\n```\n\nWait, there's a subtlety in the tag_ok check for C_terminal. I defined terminal_stop as the last three nucleotides forming a stop codon. But should I require it to be in frame? Let me reconsider. The rule says \"the insert must not contain a stop codon at the terminus before the downstream tag.\" For C-terminal fusions, the concern is an in-frame stop codon that would terminate translation before the tag. But the literal wording is \"stop codon at the terminus.\"\n\nGiven that both interpretations agree on this dataset, and for simplicity, let me use the literal \"last three nucleotides form a stop codon\" check. But actually, let me reconsider whether I should be more careful.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider. There's a potential inconsistency: for stop_ok, I require the stop codon to be in frame (length divisible by 3). For tag_ok, if I just check the last three nucleotides regardless of frame, there could be a case where the last three nucleotides form a stop codon but out of frame. In that case, stop_ok = False (not in frame) but tag_ok would also = False (s