minal) = not (insert[-3:] in STOP_CODONS), assuming len >= 3. If len < 3, there's no codon at the terminus, so no stop codon at the terminus → tag_ok = True. (Edge case not in data.)\n\nLet me code it up.\n\n```python\ndef audit_construct(row):\n    construct_id = row[\"construct_id\"].strip()\n    insert = row[\"insert_sequence\"].strip().upper()\n    tag = row[\"tag\"].strip()\n    claimed_frame = row[\"claimed_frame\"].strip()\n\n    n = len(insert)\n\n    # start_ok: insert begins with ATG.\n    start_ok = insert.startswith(\"ATG\")\n\n    # stop_ok: insert ends in an in-frame stop codon.\n    stop_ok = (n % 3 == 0) and (n >= 3) and (insert[-3:] in STOP_CODONS)\n\n    # frame_ok: length divisible by 3 AND claimed_frame is in_frame.\n    frame_ok = (n % 3 == 0) and (claimed_frame == \"in_frame\")\n\n    # tag_ok.\n    if tag.startswith(\"C_terminal_\"):\n        terminal_stop = (n >= 3) and (insert[-3:] in STOP_CODONS)\n        tag_ok = not terminal_stop\n    else:\n        tag_ok = False  # unsupported tag, fail closed\n\n    checks = {\n        \"start_ok\": start_ok,\n        \"stop_ok\": stop_ok,\n        \"frame_ok\": frame_ok,\n        \"tag_ok\": tag_ok,\n    }\n    issues = [label for key, label in CHECK_LABELS if not checks[key]]\n    overall_status = \"pass\" if not issues else \"fail\"\n\n    return {\n        \"construct_id\": construct_id,\n        \"frame_ok\": frame_ok,\n        \"start_ok\": start_ok,\n        \"stop_ok\": stop_ok,\n        \"tag_ok\": tag_ok,\n        \"overall_status\": overall_status,\n        \"issues\": \";\".join(issues),\n    }\n```\n\nCHECK_LABELS = [(\"start_ok\", \"START\"), (\"stop_ok\", \"STOP\"), (\"frame_ok\", \"FRAME\"), (\"tag_ok\", \"TAG\")].\n\nissues is built by iterating over CHECK_LABELS in order (START, STOP, FRAME, TAG) and including labels of failing checks. So for c02 (stop_ok=F, frame_ok=F), issues = [\"STOP\", \"FRAME\"] → \"STOP;FRAME\". For c03 (frame_ok=F, tag_ok=F), issues = [\"FRAME\", \"TAG\"] → \"FRAME;TAG\". For c01 (tag_ok=F), issues = [\"TAG\"]. Good.\n\nNow the main function:\n\n```python\ndef main():\n    script_dir = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    workspace_root = script_dir.parent\n    input_csv = workspace_root / \"inputs\" / \"constructs.csv\"\n    output_csv = script_dir / \"construct_audit.csv\"\n\n    rows = []\n    with open(input_csv, newline=\"\") as f:\n        reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n        for row in reader:\n            rows.append(audit_construct(row))\n\n    fieldnames = [\"construct_id\", \"frame_ok\", \"start_ok\", \"stop_ok\", \"tag_ok\", \"overall_status\", \"issues\"]\n    with open(output_csv, \"w\", newline=\"\") as f:\n        writer = csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames=fieldnames)\n        writer.writeheader()\n        for row in rows:\n            writer.writerow(row)\n\n    # Also print a summary.\n    for row in rows:\n        print(row)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nWait, but the CSV writer would write booleans as \"True\"/\"False\". Good. Let me make sure the values are actual Python booleans, and the csv writer converts them to \"True\"/\"False\". Yes.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: the csv.DictWriter would call str() on the boolean values, producing \"True\"/\"False\". Good.\n\nNow, let me reconsider the paths. The task says to write \"output/construct_audit.csv\", \"output/analysis.py\", \"output/report.md\". So output/ is a directory inside the workspace. analysis.py is inside output/. When run, analysis.py reads inputs/constructs.csv (one level up from output/) and writes output/construct_audit.csv (same directory as the script). My path logic handles this.\n\nBut wait — if analysis.py is in output/, and I run it from the workspace root as `python output/analysis.py`, then `Path(__file__).resolve().parent` = workspace/output, and `.parent` = workspace. Good. input_csv = workspace/inputs/constructs.csv. output_csv = workspace/output/construct_audit.csv.\n\nNow let me reconsider: should I also