itly say analysis.py generates report.md. But it's cleaner and more reproducible if it does. Let me have analysis.py generate both. That way, the deliverables are consistent and reproducible.\n\nLet me write analysis.py now.\n\nLet me reconsider the boolean CSV format once more. Actually, let me reconsider: I'll write booleans as \"True\"/\"False\". Let me double-check that Python's csv writer does this. Yes, csv.writer calls str() on values, and str(True) = \"True\", str(False) = \"False\".\n\nOK let me write the files.\n\nLet me write analysis.py using a heredoc or by writing directly. I'll use the exec_command tool with PowerShell to write the file. Actually, it's a multi-line Python file. Let me use a here-string in PowerShell, or use the write_stdin approach. Actually, the cleanest is to use PowerShell's Set-Content with a here-string. But the file contains special characters. Let me use a single-quoted here-string (@'...'@) to avoid variable expansion.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Writing a large Python file via PowerShell here-string is error-prone. Let me instead write it in chunks, or use a different approach.\n\nHmm, let me think. The exec_command tool runs a shell command. I can use PowerShell to write the file. Let me use @' ... '@ (single-quoted here-string) which doesn't expand variables. But I need to be careful with the here-string delimiters (the closing '@ must be at the start of the line).\n\nAlternatively, I can use Python itself to write the file, but that's circular.\n\nLet me use the approach of writing the file with PowerShell Set-Content and a here-string. Let me construct the command carefully.\n\nActually, there's a risk: the exec_command tool might have issues with very long commands or special characters. Let me write the file in a robust way. Let me use a temporary approach: write the Python file using PowerShell with a here-string.\n\nLet me draft the analysis.py content first, then figure out how to write it.\n\nLet me write the content of analysis.py:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Construct audit analysis.\n\nAudits each construct in ``inputs/constructs.csv`` for start/stop codons,\nreading frame, and tag/linker compatibility, following the frozen rules in\n``inputs/AUDIT_RULE.md``. Only fields represented in the input are checked;\nno sequence features are inferred from external sources.\n\nFrozen rules (AUDIT_RULE.md):\n  * start_ok : the insert begins with ``ATG``.\n  * stop_ok  : the insert ends in an in-frame stop codon (TAA/TAG/TGA).\n  * frame_ok : insert length is divisible by three AND claimed_frame is\n               ``in_frame``.\n  * tag_ok   : for ``C_terminal_*`` fusions the insert must not contain a\n               stop codon at the terminus before the downstream tag; other\n               tag strings are unsupported and fail closed.\n  * overall_status = pass only when all four checks are true; otherwise fail\n    and list every failing check in issues using labels START/STOP/FRAME/TAG.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport csv\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nSTOP_CODONS = {\"TAA\", \"TAG\", \"TGA\"}\n\n# Ordered (check_key, issue_label) pairs; issues are reported in this order.\nCHECK_LABELS = [\n    (\"start_ok\", \"START\"),\n    (\"stop_ok\", \"STOP\"),\n    (\"frame_ok\", \"FRAME\"),\n    (\"tag_ok\", \"TAG\"),\n]\n\nCSV_FIELDS = [\"construct_id\", \"frame_ok\", \"start_ok\", \"stop_ok\", \"tag_ok\", \"overall_status\", \"issues\"]\n\n\ndef audit_construct(row):\n    \"\"\"Apply the frozen audit rules to a single construct row.\"\"\"\n    construct_id = (row.get(\"construct_id\") or \"\").strip()\n    insert = (row.get(\"insert_sequence\") or \"\").strip().upper()\n    tag = (row.get(\"tag\") or \"\").strip()\n    claimed_frame = (row.get(\"claimed_frame\") or \"\").strip()\n\n    n = len(insert)\n\n    # start_ok: insert begins with ATG.\n    start_ok = insert.startswith(\"ATG\")\n\n    # stop_ok: insert ends in an in-frame stop codon.\n    stop_ok = (n % 3 == 0) and (n >= 3) and (insert[-3:] in STOP_CODONS)\n\n    # frame_ok: length divisible by 3 AND claimed_frame is in_frame.\n    frame_ok = (n % 3 == 0) and (claimed_frame == \"in_frame\")\n\n    # tag_ok.\n    if tag.startswith(\"C_terminal_\"):\n        terminal_stop = (n >= 3) and (insert[-3:] in STOP_CODONS)\n        tag_ok = not terminal_stop\n    else:\n        # Unsupported tag strings fail closed.\n        tag_ok = False\n\n    checks = {\n        \"start_ok\": start_ok,\n        \"stop_ok\": stop_ok,\n        \"frame_ok\": frame_ok,\n        \"tag_ok\": tag_ok,\n    }\n    issues = [label for key, label in CHECK_LABELS if not checks[key]]\n    overall_status = \"pass\" if not issues else \"fail\"\n\n    return {\n        \"construct_id\": construct_id,\n        \"frame_ok\": frame_ok,\n        \"start_ok\": start_ok,\n        \"stop_ok\": stop_ok,\n        \"tag_ok\": tag_ok,\n        \"overall_status\": overall_status,\n        \"issues\": \";\".join(issues),\n        # Extra metadata used only for reporting (not written to the CSV).\n        \"_insert\": insert,\n        \"_length\": n,\n        \"_tag\": tag,\n        \"_claimed_frame\": claimed_frame,\n        \"_promoter\": (row.get(\"promoter\") or \"\").strip(),\n    }\n\n\ndef write_csv(results, output_csv):\n    with open(output_csv, \"w\", newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        writer = csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames=CSV_FIELDS)\n        writer.writeheader()\n        for r in results:\n            writer.writerow({k: r[k] for k in CSV_FIELDS})\n\n\ndef write_report(results, report_md):\n    lines = []\n    lines.append(\"# Construct Audit Report\")\n    ...\n    with open(report_md, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        f.write(\"\\n\".join(lines))\n\n\ndef main():\n    script_dir = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    workspace_root = script_dir.parent\n    input_csv = workspace_root / \"inputs\" / \"constructs.csv\"\n    output_csv = script_dir / \"construct_audit.csv\"\n    report_md = script_dir / \"report.md\"\n\n    with open(input_csv, newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n        results = [audit_construct(row) for row in reader]\n\n    write_csv(results, output_csv)\n    write_report(results, report_md)\n\n    # Console summary.\n    for r in results:\n        print(f\"{r['construct_id']}: {r['overall_status']} issues=[{r['issues']}]\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nLet me flesh out write_report. Let me include the rules and a summary table plus per-construct details.\n\nLet me write write_report fully:\n\n```python\ndef write_report(results, report_md):\n    total = len(results)\n    n_pass = sum(1 for r in results if r[\"overall_status\"] == \"pass\")\n    n_fail = total - n_pass\n\n    lines = []\n    lines.append(\"# Construct Audit Report\")\n    lines.append(\"\")\n    lines.append(\"Audit of construct sequences per the frozen rules in `inputs/AUDIT_RULE.md`. \"\n                 \"Only fields represented in `inputs/constructs.csv` are checked; no sequence \"\n                 \"features are inferred.\")\n    lines.append(\"\")\n    lines.append(\"## Rules applied\")\n    lines.append(\"\")\n    lines.append(\"| Check | Rule |\")\n    lines.append(\"|-------|------|\")\n    lines.append(\"| `start_ok` | Insert begins with `ATG`. |\")\n    lines.append(\"| `stop_ok` | Insert ends in an in-frame stop codon (`TAA`/`TAG`/`TGA`). |\")\n    lines.append(\"| `frame_ok` | Insert length divisible by 3 **and** `claimed_frame` is `in_frame`. |\")\n    lines.append(\"| `tag_ok` | For `C_terminal_*` fusions the insert must not contain a stop codon at the terminus before the downstream tag; other tag strings are unsupported and fail closed. |\")\n    lines.append(\"| `overall_status` | `pass` only when all four checks are true; otherwise `fail` and every failing check is listed in `issues`. |\")\n    lines.append(\"\")\n    lines.append(\"## Summary\")\n    lines.append(\"\")\n    lines.append(f\"- Constructs audited: {total}\")\n    lines.append(f\"- Pass: {n_pass}\")\n    lines.append(f\"- Fail: {n_fail}\")\n    lines.append(\"\")\n    lines.append(\"## Results\")\n    lines.append(\"\")\n    lines.append(\"| construct_id | frame_ok | start_ok | stop_ok | tag_ok | overall_status | issues |\")\n    lines.append(\"|--------------|----------|----------|---------|--------|----------------|--------|\")\n    for r in results:\n        lines.append(\n            f\"| {r['construct_id']} | {r['frame_ok']} | {r['start_ok']} | {r['stop_ok']} \"\n            f\"| {r['tag_ok']} | {r['overall_status']} | {r['issues'] or '-'} |\"\n        )\n    lines.append(\"\")\n    lines.append(\"## Per-construct detail\")\n    for r in results:\n        lines.append(\"\")\n        lines.append(f\"### {r['construct_id']}\")\n        lines.append(\"\")\n        lines.append(f\"- promoter: `{r['_promoter']}`\")\n        lines.append(f\"- tag: `{r['_tag']}`\")\n        lines.append(f\"- claimed_frame: `{r['_claimed_frame']}`\")\n        lines.append(f\"- insert_sequence: `{r['_insert']}`\")\n        lines.append(f\"- insert length: {r['_length']} (length % 3 = {r['_length'] % 3})\")\n        lines.append(f\"- start_ok: {r['start_ok']}\")\n        lines.append(f\"- stop_ok: {r['stop_ok']}\")\n        lines.append(f\"- frame_ok: {r['frame_ok']}\")\n        lines.append(f\"- tag_ok: {r['tag_ok']}\")\n        lines.append(f\"- overall_status: **{r['overall_status']}**\")\n        lines.append(f\"- issues: {r['issues'] or '(none)'}\")\n        # Explanation\n        lines.append(\"\")\n        lines.append(\"Explanation:\")\n        for expl in explain(r):\n            lines.append(f\"  - {expl}\")\n    lines.append(\"\")\n\n    with open(report_md, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        f.write(\"\\n\".join(lines))\n```\n\nAnd explain(r) returns a list of explanation strings for each failing check. Let me write explain:\n\n```python\ndef explain(r):\n    out = []\n    if not r[\"start_ok\"]:\n        out.append(\"START: insert does not begin with ATG.\")\n    if not r[\"stop_ok\"]:\n        if r[\"_length\"] % 3 != 0:\n            out.append(f\"STOP: insert length {r['_length']} is not divisible by 3, so it cannot end in an in-frame stop codon.\")\n        else:\n            out.append(f\"STOP: terminal codon `{r['_insert'][-3:]}` is not a stop codon (TAA/TAG/TGA).\")\n    if not r[\"frame_ok\"]:\n        reasons = []\n        if r[\"_length\"] % 3 != 0:\n            reasons.append(f\"length {r['_length']} not divisible by 3\")\n        if r[\"_claimed_frame\"] != \"in_frame\":\n            reasons.append(f\"claimed_frame is `{r['_claimed_frame']}` (not `in_frame`)\")\n        out.append(\"FRAME: \" + \" and \".join(reasons) + \".\")\n    if not r[\"tag_ok\"]:\n        if r[\"_tag\"].startswith(\"C_terminal_\"):\n            out.append(f\"TAG: `C_terminal_*` fusion but the insert ends in stop codon `{r['_insert'][-3:]}`, which would terminate translation before the downstream tag.\")\n        else:\n            out.append(f\"TAG: tag `{r['_tag']}` is unsupported; fails closed.\")\n    if not out:\n        out.append(\"All checks passed.\")\n    return out\n```\n\nOK this is getting detailed. Let me finalize and write the file.\n\nNow let me reconsider the CSV boolean format once more. I'll go with \"True\"/\"False\".\n\nNow, how to write this file via exec_command? Let me use PowerShell with a here-string. The challenge is that the Python code contains both single and double quotes, backticks, etc. A single-quoted here-string (@'...'@) in PowerShell treats everything literally (no variable expansion, no escaping), except that the closing '@ must be at the start of a line. Since my Python code doesn't contain a line starting with '@', this should work. But wait, does my Python code contain '@'? Let me check. The decorator '@' — I don't use decorators. So no line starts with '@'. Good. But single-quoted here-strings can't contain '@ followed by a quote... 